- the ceiling \(V/(V+E/n)\), which increases strictly in \(n\) and tends to 1;
- the one-feature law \(R^2=\text{ceiling}\cdot\rho^2\), with Cauchy–Schwarz obtained from least squares;
- the fraction-of-ceiling law and the identity \(1-R^2=\kappa(1-\text{ceiling})\);
- the residue count's exact mean and variance, and orthogonality across primes via the Chinese Remainder Theorem;
- the exact \((p-1)/2 : (p-1)/2\) split showing the feature can't see the factors;
- the empirical table, algorithms, applications and future work.

Neither document mentions the formal development.

The consistency check matters for anyone citing the numbers. At \(u=3.5\) the reported figures fit together: they force \(\kappa\in(1.12,1.13)\). At \(u=2.5\) they don't quite: with \(\kappa\le1.24\), the ceiling would have to be below 0.436, not 0.45. Either the ceiling and the held-out \(R^2\) were computed on different data, or \(\kappa\) there is nearer 1.27. Both papers and the article state this.

